Next.js -> NestJS Bearer Token
Handoff
Sending the session access token without leaking it into URLs or logs

Phase 18 trust boundary

Next.js authenticated session

Authorization: Bearer <access token>

NestJS authentication guard

Verified Supabase identity

Controller and business service

Course principle: a user ID becomes trusted only after the access token has been cryptographically
verified by the backend.

The HTTP contract
GET /auth/me HTTP/1.1
Host: 127.0.0.1:4000
Authorization: Bearer <SUPABASE_ACCESS_TOKEN>

The access token belongs in the standard Authorization header. It should not be placed in query
strings, route parameters, analytics events, or logs.

Server-side Next.js helper
// apps/web/src/lib/api/authenticated-fetch.ts
import { createClient } from '@/lib/supabase/server'

export async function authenticatedApiFetch(
path: string,
init: RequestInit = {},
) {
const supabase = await createClient()
const { data, error } = await supabase.auth.getSession()

if (error || !data.session?.access_token) {
throw new Error('Authenticated session required')
}

const headers = new Headers(init.headers)
headers.set(
'Authorization',
`Bearer ${data.session.access_token}`,
)

return fetch(
`${process.env.API_BASE_URL}${path}`,
{ ...init, headers, cache: 'no-store' },
)
}

Phase 17 taught the difference between trusting a session and merely reading cookie contents. This
helper is used only after the server-side auth layer has established the current session.

Browser-side calls
A browser Client Component can also obtain the current session access token from Supabase and call
NestJS. That path is subject to CORS because the browser is crossing origins.
const { data } = await supabase.auth.getSession()

await fetch('http://127.0.0.1:4000/auth/me', {
headers: {
Authorization:
`Bearer ${data.session?.access_token}`,
},
})

Do not do this
/auth/me?token=eyJ...

console.log(accessToken)

localStorage.setItem('debug-token', accessToken)

Bearer tokens are credentials. Treat them like secrets during their lifetime.

