Protect Routes Deliberately
Per-route, controller-wide, and auth-by-default strategies

Phase 18 trust boundary

Next.js authenticated session

Authorization: Bearer <access token>

NestJS authentication guard

Verified Supabase identity

Controller and business service

Course principle: a user ID becomes trusted only after the access token has been cryptographically
verified by the backend.

Start explicit
@Controller('tasks')
@UseGuards(SupabaseAuthGuard)
export class TasksController {
// every endpoint in this controller is authenticated
}

Controller-wide protection is easy to see and fits TaskFlow while we are still learning the boundary.

Public endpoints remain public
@Controller('health')
export class HealthController {
@Get()
check() {
return { status: 'ok' }
}
}

Global guard later

NestJS supports registering an authentication guard globally and using metadata such as @Public()
for anonymous routes. This can be safer when most of an API should be protected, because forgetting
a decorator no longer accidentally creates a public endpoint.
providers: [
{
provide: APP_GUARD,
useClass: SupabaseAuthGuard,
},
]

Why we do not switch globally yet
• We want the trust boundary visible while learning.

• Phase 19 will add authorization and may influence guard composition.

• Public-vs-protected route inventory should be explicit before default-deny is enabled.

Recommended production direction
For an internal business system where almost every endpoint is private, authenticated by default with a
small explicit public-route escape hatch is usually easier to audit than protecting hundreds of routes one
by one.

